{
  "raw": {
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189733": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Finance",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Business Location: College of Business, Stony Brook University Position Description The College of Business at Stony Brook University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track or tenured faculty position in Finance at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor level, beginning Fall 2027. The teaching load and research support are comparable to reputable public research institutions. Salary is commensurate with qualifications, rank, publication record, experience, and relevant expertise . We especially encourage applications from experienced scholars with excellent publication records in premier finance journals, particularly those on the Financial Times journal list. About Stony Brook University Stony Brook is New York State’s flagship public research university and has been ranked the #1 public university in New York by US News & World Report in recent years. It is part of the State University of New York (SUNY) system, a center of academic excellence and an internationally recognized research institution that offers all students a world-class education. Stony Brook University is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU) and is classified as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Foundation, reflecting the highest level of research activity. As a leading institution in STEM, health sciences, and interdisciplinary research, Stony Brook offers faculty a rich, collaborative environment. About the College of Business The College of Business at Stony Brook University is committed to promoting high-quality research and discoveries, as well as delivering a transformative business education that prepares students for leadership roles in an evolving, technology-driven business landscape. We foster a welcoming, respectful, and quality research environment by cultivating a community of productive and collaborative scholars. We value interdisciplinary collaboration, an inclusive learning environment, and we encourage faculty to incorporate real-world applications that emphasize ethics, innovation, and societal impact. The College offers a combination of graduate (masters and MBAs) and undergraduate degree programs to over 2,600 students, making it a vibrant academic community. Required Qualifications: Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Finance or a closely related field from an AACSB accredited institution. Candidates must possess at least three years of experience as a tenure-track/tenured faculty member or a researcher at a reputable U.S. institution . Assistant Professor candidates should demonstrate a promising research pipeline with strong potential for publication in top-tier journals; Associate and Full Professor candidates must present an established record of publications in the highest quality finance and business journals, particularly those on the Financial Times journal list . Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated record of sustained research productivity and a strong external reputation in the field. Proven ability to teach finance courses effectively across multiple levels (undergraduate, MBA, master's, etc.) Evidence of prior leadership experience or a clear willingness to contribute to College’s initiatives, faculty governance, and the broader academic mission of the college. Application Instructions To receive full consideration, candidates should upload: Cover letter Curriculum vita Copies of research papers Teaching evaluations Research statement Teaching statement Service statement Contact Information for three references (recommendation letters will be solicited for finalists) All application materials will be accepted until the position is closed and must be submitted electronically online through Interfolio. Ple ase use the Apply Now button to begin your application. For technical support, please visit Interfolio’s Support Site  ( https://support.interfolio.com/ ) or reach out to their Scholar Service Team at help@interfollio.com or (877) 997-8807."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193151": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Finance",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Business Location: Stony Brook University College of Business The College of Business at Stony Brook University is seeking applicants for an Assistant professor position in Finance. This is a full-time appointment in the College’s Finance area. The position starts on September 1, 2027. Salary, teaching load, and research support are commensurate with qualifications, academic record, and experience. Candidates in all areas of finance are encouraged to apply. We particularly welcome research that explores applications of artificial intelligence in Finance. About Stony Brook University Stony Brook, one of America’s most dynamic public universities, is New York’s flagship university and No. 1 public university ranked by US News in recent years. Stony Brook is part of the State University of New York (SUNY) system, a center of academic excellence and an internationally recognized research institution that offers all students a world-class education. Stony Brook University is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU) and is classified as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Foundation. About the College of Business Stony Brook University's College of Business is committed to providing outstanding and accessible business education to empower future business leaders. We value a diverse and inclusive learning environment and encourage faculty to incorporate experiential learning to foster creativity, teamwork, and networking while emphasizing ethics and the common good. We offer a combination of graduate (masters and MBAs) and undergraduate programs to over 2,500 students. Required Qualifications: Candidates must hold a Ph.D. (or foreign equivalent) in Finance or a related field by the time of appointment. Candidates must demonstrate strong potential to produce high-quality research publishable in leading finance journals, particularly those included in the Financial Times 50 (FT50) list. We welcome candidates from all areas of finance. Preferred Qualifications: Research synergy with our finance faculty in the areas of corporate finance, investments, behavioral finance, international finance, banking, and quantitative finance. Evidence of excellent research productivity by a portfolio of publications in the highest quality finance journals. Application Materials: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Job market paper Research statement Teaching statement Teaching evaluations Three letters of recommendation Application Instructions: Application materials must be submitted electronically through Interfolio and should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, job market paper, research statement, teaching statement, teaching evaluations, and three letters of recommendation. Applications received by November 30, 2026, will receive full consideration."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195203": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Practice/ Lecturer position in Analytics",
      "description": "Posted: Oct 08, 2026 Deadline: Nov 05, 2026 Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Business Location: College of Business, Stony Brook University The College of Business at Stony Brook University (SBU) invites applications for a full-time non-tenure track Assistant/Associate Professor of Practice/Lecturer position in Analytics starting in Fall 2027. The Operations and Decision Analytics area (the home area) at the College of Business includes faculty with diverse research interests and backgrounds. We value a collegial environment and encourage interdisciplinary research. For more information about the Operations and Decision Analytics area, please visit https://www.stonybrook.edu/commcms/business/faculty-research/_research/opresearch-analytics.php . We are committed to fostering a welcoming and respectful academic environment by cultivating a community of scholars where all individuals feel valued. Recruiting faculty with a wide range of talents, skills, backgrounds, and experiences is a key priority. Candidates are expected to demonstrate a clear dedication to this goal by working collaboratively with colleagues to ensure broad access to opportunities, support a variety of viewpoints, and contribute to a climate of mutual respect. About the College of Business: The College of Business at Stony Brook University is committed to delivering a transformative business education that prepares students for leadership roles in an evolving, technology-driven business landscape. We value interdisciplinary collaboration, an inclusive learning environment, and encourage faculty to incorporate real-world applications that emphasize ethics, innovation, and societal impact. The College offers a combination of graduate (Masters of Science and MBAs) and undergraduate programs to over 2500 students, making it a vibrant academic community. About Stony Brook University: Stony Brook is New York State’s flagship public research university and has been ranked the #1 public university in New York by US News & World Report in recent years. It is part of the State University of New York (SUNY) system, a center of academic excellence and an internationally recognized research institution that offers all students a world-class education. Stony Brook University is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU) and is classified as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Foundation, reflecting the highest level of research activity. As a leading institution in STEM, health sciences, and interdisciplinary research, Stony Brook offers faculty a rich, collaborative environment. Responsibilities and Requirements ● Develop and teach Business Analytics, Machine Learning, Operations Management, Information Systems, or other related courses at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. The expected teaching load is three courses (professor of practice) or four courses (lecturer) per semester. ● Perform research activities, as per academic rank (publishing peer-reviewed papers, presenting at conferences, etc.), to maintain the AACSB scholarly active requirements. ● Help establish industry connections and create opportunities for student and faculty interactions. ● Organize student clubs and other extracurricular activities (mentoring students’ internships, case competitions, etc.) in operations and analytics. ● Participate in the administrative and committee assignments (e.g., assurance of learning and AACSB accreditation, organizing research seminars, etc.) and contribute to the SBU community. Required Qualifications ● Be eligible to work in the United States without a visa sponsorship ● A track record of excellent teaching business analytics, machine learning, database, operations management, supply chain management, or other related courses in different modalities. ● A Ph.D. (or foreign equivalent) from an accredited program in business analytics–related field (Operations Management, Operations Research, Information Systems, or other related fields) is required for the Professor of Practice position, while a Master’s degree from an accredited program in one of the same fields is required for the Lecturer position. Preferred Qualifications ● Experience in industry engagement and effectively connecting with diverse industries, fostering partnerships, or collaborating with external stakeholders. ● Published and/or potential to publish in peer-reviewed business journals in the use of analytics. ● A track record of excellent teaching business analytics, machine learning, database, operations management, supply chain management, or other related courses in different modalities. Applicants should submit the following documents via Interfolio: ● Cover Letter ● Curriculum Vitae ● Teaching Philosophy Statement ● Service Statement ● Teaching Evaluations ● Research Statement (Optional) ● Research Papers (Optional) ● Names and contact information for three references (Recommendation letters will be requested from finalists) All application materials will be accepted until the position is closed and must be submitted electronically online through Interfolio. Please use the Apply Now button to begin your application. For technical support, please visit Interfolio’s Support Site ( https://support.interfolio.com/ ) or reach out to their Scholar Service Team at help@interfollio.com or (877) 997-8807."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193727": {
      "title": "Milnor Lecturer",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: Centers & Institutes: Institute for Mathematical Sciences Location: Stony Brook, NY The Institute for Mathematical Sciences (IMS) at Stony Brook University seeks applications for one-two postdoctoral positions beginning in the Fall 2027 semester. The appointments will be for two to three years with a competitive salary and reduced teaching. Our 1,100 acre campus is home to 24,000 undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students, and more than 13,500 faculty and staff, including those employed at Stony Brook University Medical Center, Suffolk County’s only academic medical center. The University is a member of the Association of American Universities and co-manager of nearby Brookhaven National Laboratory (BNL), a multidisciplinary research laboratory supporting world class scientific programs. The recently established Simons Center for Geometry and Physics, located next to the math building, runs intensive programs on the interface between mathematics and theoretical physics, over one thousand scientists pass through the SCGP annually. Many opportunities exist for collaborative research, and in some cases, joint appointments can be arranged. Home to many highly ranked graduate research programs, Stony Brook University is located 60 miles from New York City on Long Island’s scenic north shore. The Long Island Rail Road connects the Stony Brook Station on campus to midtown Manhattan. Please be sure to apply to the following positions in the Simons Center for Geometry and Physics and the Mathematics Department as well: Research Assistant Professor - The Simons Center for Geometry & Physics has two open positions for Mathematics Research Assistant Professors available with a starting date of September 1, 2026. These are non-tenure track appointments offering an annual salary of $85,000. A research fund of $5,000 will be offered for scientiﬁc travel, inviting visitors and other research related expenses on a yearly basis; in addition, $2,000 will be provided to defray moving expenses in the ﬁrst year. The Simons Center for Geometry and Physics is an endowed research organization devoted to furthering fundamental knowledge in geometry and theoretical physics; especially knowledge at the interface of these two disciplines. Please click here to apply. ( https://apply.interfolio.com/193704) Simons Lecturers - The Mathematics Department at Stony Brook University expects to have two Simons Instructor ( SUNY title of Lecturer) positions available beginning in the fall semester of 2027 contingent on funding availability.  A Simons Instructor position is a three-year post-doctoral level appointment with a competitive salary of $75,000 and reduced teaching. Please click here to apply ( https://apply.interfolio.com/194108 ). Required Qualifications: Ph.D. degree (or foreign equivalent) in Mathematics before 8/31/2027. Preferred Qualifications: Candidates whose research areas complement those of current department members. All application materials must be submitted online through Interfolio. Please use the Apply Now button to begin your application. Candidates must upload: 1)  AMS standard cover sheet 2) Cover Letter 3) Curriculum Vitae including Publication List 4) Three Reference letters 5) Research and Teaching Statement. Applications should be received by November 14, 2026. Stony Brook University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity employer. We encourage protected veterans, individuals with disabilities, women and minorities to apply. Pursuant to Executive Order 161, no State entity, as defined by the Executive Order, is permitted to ask, or mandate, in any form, that an applicant for employment provide his or her current compensation, or any prior compensation history, until such time as the applicant is extended a conditional offer of employment with compensation.  If such information has been requested from you before such time, please contact the Governor’s Office of Employee Relations at (518) 474-6988 or via email at info@goer.ny.gov. Stony Brook University is 100% tobacco-free as of January 1, 2016. See our policy and learn more at stonybrook.edu/tobaccofree. The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632- 6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193590": {
      "title": "Endowed Faculty Position in Physical and Quantitative Biology",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: Centers & Institutes: Laufer Center for Physical and Quantitative Biology Location: Laufer Center for Physical & Quantitative Biology We invite applications for an endowed tenure-track faculty position in Physical and Quantitative Biology at the Laufer Center at Stony Brook University. We seek candidates at the assistant or associate professor level.  We're open to all areas of physical and quantitative biology, theory/computations and/or experiments (see our webpage Research Overview — The Laufer Center ) but are particularly interested in molecular and cellular systems dynamics, interaction networks, adaptation/evolution, drug discovery and design, and development and applications of AI-driven mechanistic studies of molecular-to-multicellular interactions and dynamics. The position focuses on research, with limited teaching, and is supported by a Laufer Endowed Professorship , which provides annual research support. The successful candidate will have considerable freedom to choose the departmental affiliation that best fits their research areas. The successful candidate will have a PhD in life sciences, physical sciences, engineering, computer science, or mathematics; a strong record of research productivity; and the potential for collaboration and leadership in a research setting. The Louis and Beatrice Laufer Center for Physical and Quantitative Biology at Stony Brook University is a multidisciplinary hub for research and graduate training that brings together experts in physics, mathematics, computer science, chemistry, and biology for discovery in biomolecules and cells and to advance drug discovery. Stony Brook University is a flagship of the State University of New York (SUNY) system, and among the top 25 public universities in the nation. Located 60 miles from New York City on the beautiful wooded North Shore of Long Island Sound, Stony Brook University has a Medical School and is located near Brookhaven National Laboratory and the Cold Spring Harbor research labs. Stony Brook University invites applications from all qualified individuals. The University is an equal opportunity educator and employer, committed to compliance with all applicable laws and regulations governing fair employment practices. Required qualifications: PhD (or foreign equivalent) in life sciences, physical sciences, engineering, computer science, or mathematics. Strong record of research productivity; and potential for collaboration and leadership in a research setting. Preferred qualifications: Quality of past and current research. Demonstrated leadership. Quality of future proposed research. Fundability of proposed research. Complementarity of research with Laufer Center interests and potential for collaborations. Promise of scientific research and the degree to which it aligns with the Center’s research vision. Strong letters of recommendation. Applicants should arrange to have three letters of recommendation sent to us and should submit the following materials: (1) a cover letter, (2) CV, (3) summary of past research activities (up to 2 pages), (4) overview of future research interests (up to 2 pages). The deadline for applications is November 16, 2026. Electronic submission through Interfolio is required. Questions can be addressed to Sabrina Kralik (sabrina.kralik@stonybrook.edu). Stony Brook University is committed to creating and maintaining a workplace and educational environment that is safe, accessible, and free of all forms of discrimination, sexual misconduct or research misconduct, among other infractions. In support of this commitment, certain candidates for employment will be required to disclose such employment-related misconduct findings and pending investigations or proceedings, and final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions will authorize their current and previous employer(s) from the last seven (7) years to disclose such information to the University. Employment is contingent on your full and complete disclosure on these matters. In the event that you fail to disclose any such matter or in the event of an unsatisfactory outcome of the disclosure and review process, an offer of employment may be revoked at SBU's sole discretion. If SBU becomes aware of a failure to disclose or misrepresentation of any such matter after your employment commences, you may be subject to discipline, up to and including termination. The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632- 6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/126603": {
      "title": "Tenured Physics Professor - Simons Center for Geometry & Physics",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: Centers & Institutes: Simons Center for Geometry and Physics Location: Stony Brook, NY The Simons Center for Geometry and Physics at Stony Brook University seeks applications for one Tenured Professor position in Theoretical Physics; the candidate will also be a Tenured Professor at the YITP/Department of Physics and Astronomy. The search is open to outstanding candidates in any field of theoretical physics, ranging from Quantum Gravity to Condensed Matter physics. The applicant does not necessarily need to be tenured or be in a tenure track position at another institution. This individual is expected to engage in research characterized by creativity and vision, and in mentorship with a commitment to inclusivity. There are no teaching obligations or heavy administration duties required in this position, however, there will be ample resources for research. The faculty member is expected to be involved in selecting and mentoring postdocs as well. The Simons Center for Geometry and Physics has close ties with the C.N. Yang Institute for Theoretical Physics (YITP) and the Physics and Mathematics departments, and is devoted to furthering fundamental knowledge in mathematics and theoretical physics, especially knowledge at the interface of these two disciplines. Stony Brook University, one of four research-intensive campuses within the State University of New York (SUNY) system, is widely regarded as its flagship. The University embraces its mission to provide comprehensive undergraduate, graduate and professional education of the highest quality, and its rankings bear that out. It’s included among the top 1% of universities in the world by the 2018 QS World University Rankings and among the top 40 public universities by U.S.News & World Report’s 2020 Best Colleges rankings. It is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities, composed of the top 62 research institutions in North America. As Long Island’s largest single-site employer, Stony Brook has nearly 15,000 full- and part-time employees, including more than 2,700 faculty. It enrolled roughly 26,800 students in fall 2019 — 17,900 undergraduate students and 8,900 graduate students — and offers more than 200 majors, minors and combined-degree programs. The Department of Athletics supports 18 Division I varsity intercollegiate athletic programs that compete at the highest level within the NCAA. Located approximately 60 miles east of Manhattan on Long Island’s beautiful North Shore, Stony Brook is situated on 1,454 wooded acres, encompassing 13 schools and colleges; a Research and Development Park; world-class athletics facilities, including an 8,300-seat stadium and a 4,000-seat arena; and Stony Brook Medicine, Long Island’s premier academic medical center. Also part of the University is a teaching and research campus in Southampton, New York, which offers graduate arts programs and is the site of the Marine Sciences Center. In addition, Stony Brook has a role in running, and performs joint research with, Brookhaven National Laboratory, the only Department of Energy Laboratory in the Northeast. Home to the Emerson String Quartet, the Pollock-Krasner House in East Hampton, NY, and the Humanities Institute, with endeavors that extend to the Turkana Basin Institute in Kenya and the Ranomafana National Park in Madagascar, Stony Brook sustains an international reputation that cuts across the arts, humanities, social sciences and natural sciences. Required Qualifications: PhD degree or foreign equivalent in theoretical physics or closely related field, with an established record of outstanding independent research. To receive full consideration candidates should upload: 1) Cover letter 2) Curriculum Vitae 3) Research statement *4) Diversity, equity, and inclusion statement *5) List of publications All application materials must be submitted online. Please use the Apply Now button to begin your application. For technical support, please visit Interfolio's Support Site ( https://support.interfolio.com/ ) or reach out to their Scholar Service Team at help@interfollio.com or (877) 997-8807. The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632- 6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/126937": {
      "title": "Tenured Mathematics Professor - Simons Center for Geometry & Physics",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: Centers & Institutes: Simons Center for Geometry and Physics Location: Stony Brook, NY The Simons Center for Geometry and Physics at Stony Brook University seeks applications for one Tenured Professor position in Mathematics (https://scgp.stonybrook.edu); the candidate will also be a Tenured Professor at the Mathematics department. The search is open to outstanding candidates in the area of geometry understood in a broad sense and related areas. The applicant does not necessarily need to be tenured or be in a tenure track position at another institution. This individual is expected to engage in research characterized by creativity and vision, and in mentorship with a commitment to inclusivity. There are no teaching obligations or heavy administration duties required in this position, however, there will be ample resources for research. The faculty member is expected to be involved in selecting and mentoring postdocs as well. The Simons Center for Geometry and Physics has close ties with the C.N. Yang Institute for Theoretical Physics (YITP) and the Physics and Mathematics departments, and is devoted to furthering fundamental knowledge in mathematics and theoretical physics, especially knowledge at the interface of these two disciplines. Stony Brook University, one of four research-intensive campuses within the State University of New York (SUNY) system, is widely regarded as its flagship. The University embraces its mission to provide comprehensive undergraduate, graduate and professional education of the highest quality, and its rankings bear that out. It’s included among the top 1% of universities in the world by the 2018 QS World University Rankings and among the top 40 public universities by U.S.News & World Report’s 2020 Best Colleges rankings. It is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities, composed of the top 62 research institutions in North America. As Long Island’s largest single-site employer, Stony Brook has nearly 15,000 full- and part-time employees, including more than 2,700 faculty. It enrolled roughly 26,800 students in fall 2019 — 17,900 undergraduate students and 8,900 graduate students — and offers more than 200 majors, minors and combined-degree programs. The Department of Athletics supports 18 Division I varsity intercollegiate athletic programs that compete at the highest level within the NCAA. Located approximately 60 miles east of Manhattan on Long Island’s beautiful North Shore, Stony Brook is situated on 1,454 wooded acres, encompassing 13 schools and colleges; a Research and Development Park; world-class athletics facilities, including an 8,300-seat stadium and a 4,000-seat arena; and Stony Brook Medicine, Long Island’s premier academic medical center. Also part of the University is a teaching and research campus in Southampton, New York, which offers graduate arts programs and is the site of the Marine Sciences Center. In addition, Stony Brook has a role in running, and performs joint research with, Brookhaven National Laboratory, the only Department of Energy Laboratory in the Northeast. Home to the Emerson String Quartet, the Pollock-Krasner House in East Hampton, NY, and the Humanities Institute, with endeavors that extend to the Turkana Basin Institute in Kenya and the Ranomafana National Park in Madagascar, Stony Brook sustains an international reputation that cuts across the arts, humanities, social sciences and natural sciences. Required Qualifications: PhD degree or foreign equivalent in geometry or closely related field, with an established record of outstanding independent research. To receive full consideration candidates should upload: 1) Cover letter 2) Curriculum Vitae 3) Research statement *4) Diversity, equity, and inclusion statement All application materials must be submitted online. Please use the Apply Now button to begin your application. For technical support, please visit Interfolio's Support Site ( https://support.interfolio.com/ ) or reach out to their Scholar Service Team at help@interfollio.com or (877) 997-8807. The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632- 6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police . If you need a disability-related accommodation, please contact the Office of Equity and Access at (631) 632-6280."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193704": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Professor, Simons Center for Geometry & Physics - Mathematics (Non-Tenure Track Postdoc Position)",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: Centers & Institutes: Simons Center for Geometry and Physics Location: Stony Brook, NY The Simons Center for Geometry & Physics has two open positions for Mathematics Research Assistant Professors available, with a starting date of September 1, 2027. These are non-tenure track appointments offering an annual salary of $85,000. A research fund of of $5,000 will be offered for scientific travel, inviting visitors, and other research-related expenses, on a yearly basis; in addition $2,000 will be provided to defray moving expenses in the first year. In this position, you will be required to teach a total of 5 courses over 6 semesters . We seek candidates with an outstanding research potential in the field of geometry, in the broadest sense. The Simons Center for Geometry and Physics is an endowed research organization devoted to furthering fundamental knowledge in geometry and theoretical physics; especially knowledge at the interface of these two disciplines. Please be sure to apply to following positions in the Mathematics and IMS departments as well : Mathematics Department: Simons Lecturer - https://apply.interfolio.com/194108 Stony Brook Hefei Lecturer - https://apply.interfolio.com/195002 IMS: Milnor Lecturer - https://apply.interfolio.com/193727 Stony Brook University, one of four research-intensive campuses within the State University of New York (SUNY) system, is widely regarded as its flagship. The University embraces its mission to provide comprehensive undergraduate, graduate and professional education of the highest quality. Required Qualifications : Applicants are required to have earned a Ph.D. degree (or foreign equivalent) in Mathematics before August 31, 2027. Established record of outstanding independent research. Preferred Qualifications: Preference will be given to those that have received their Ph.D. degree (or foreign equivalent) no earlier than 2022. Complete applications should include the following: 1) AMS Standard Cover Sheet 2) Cover Letter 3) Curriculum Vitae 4) Publication List 5) Research Statement 6) Teaching Statement 7) Three (3) Reference Letters Only electronic submissions will be accepted. Please use the Apply Now button to begin your application.  For technical support, please visit Interfolio's Support Site (https://support.interfolio.com/) or reach out to their Scholar Service Team at help@interfollio.com or (877) 997-8807.  For questions regarding this position, please contact Melissa Wessler at mwessler@scgp.stonybrook.edu. The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632-6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193344": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor of Indian Sociolinguistics and Multilingualism and Director of the Mattoo Center for India Studies",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: Asian and Asian -American Studies Location: Stony Brook University The Department of Asian and Asian American Studies at Stony Brook University invites applications for a tenured faculty position at the rank of Full or Associate Professor, beginning Fall 2027. We are looking for an outstanding scholar with a solid record of publications and an international reputation in the area of Indian languages and society, including sociolinguistics and multilingualism. The successful candidate will also serve as Director of the Mattoo Center for India Studies. The successful candidate will hold the academic rank of a tenured Full or Associate Professor in the Department of Asian and Asian American Studies. They will teach two undergraduate or graduate courses per semester (of which one course will be released per year for directing the Center), in areas such as, but not limited to: Language and Society in South Asia, Multilingualism, Language Contact, Introduction to Indian Civilization, Language in the Study of India, English in India and World Englishes. They will complement existing scholarship on India/South Asia in the department and contribute to the undergraduate major in AAAS and the Minor in South Asian Studies, the two master’s programs (in Asian and Asian American Studies and in the History of Philosophy, East and West), the doctoral programs in related departments. They will collaborate with and contribute to academic units, such as the Mattoo Center for India Studies and the Nirmal and Augustina Mattoo Endowed Chair in Classical Indic Humanities. The successful candidate will also serve as Director of the Bishembarnath and Sheela Mattoo Center for India Studies, an internationally respected academic resource and a vibrant community asset, dedicated to promoting a better appreciation of India’s contribution to human knowledge for the benefit of the university and the community.  Over the last 30 years, the Center has exposed over 25,000 students to various aspects of India.  Supported by the Center’s endowments, the Director will provide academic leadership and strategic direction to the Center by conducting research projects, including facilitating faculty research with grants and collaboration with scholars in India; teaching of courses on India; organizing seminars, conferences and endowed lectures; administering scholarships and travel grants; developing the India Reference Library; publishing books and journals; conducting outreach programs; and advancing a robust engagement with the public and the Indian American community in the New York metropolitan area to grow the Center’s resources. Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Stony Brook is a dynamic research university, a member of the Association of American Universities, with a large, diverse, intercultural student population. Required Qualifications Ph.D. from a reputed university in South Asian Studies, Linguistics, Literature, or a related field, focused on the languages of India, with reference to sociolinguistics, multilingualism, translation, or related topics; Evidence of a strong research program, quality publications, and high standing in the international research community; Deep knowledge of Indian civilization and society, its cultural roots and global impact, as well as issues and developments in India Studies; Effective teaching experience at the university level; Knowledge of at least one of the languages of India. Demonstrated administrative experience and leadership potential in a university context; Effective engagement with Indian American communities in terms of outreach and advancement; Eligible to work in the United States without a visa sponsorship. Preferred Qualifications Knowledge of Sanskrit, classical India, and Indian knowledge systems Experience in building (or potential to build) a vibrant India Studies program Experience in working with archives in India; Interest and experience in organizing a Study Abroad program in India Application Instructions To apply for this position, please submit the following: *a cover letter describing the candidate’s qualifications and vision; *a curriculum vitae; *a teaching statement; *two representative publications; *a statement of research program; *names and emails of three referees (just names and email addresses; we will contact them at the appropriate stage.) Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. For further information about this position, please contact Professor S. N. Sridhar at s.sridhar@stonybrook.edu . For questions regarding the application process, please contact Ms. Theresa Spadola at t heresa.spadola@stonybrook.edu or call 631-632-7690. Stony Brook University/SUNY is an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193424": {
      "title": "Lecturer 10 - Chinese Language",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: Asian and Asian -American Studies Location: Department of Asian and Asian American Studies, Stony Brook University Description : The Department of Asian and Asian American Studies in the College of Arts and Sciences at Stony Brook University invites applications for a full-time Lecturer in Chinese language to commence Fall 2027. This is a full-time, non-tenure-track term appointment, renewable subject to satisfactory performance, programmatic need, and available funding. Stony Brook’s Chinese program serves one of the most diverse undergraduate populations in the country. It teaches a full sequence from the true beginner through advanced levels, offers dedicated sections for heritage learners, and supports the Minor in China Studies. The successful candidate will teach across that sequence [3 courses per semester]; prepare syllabi, materials, and assessments and maintain common standards across sections; advise students; participate in placement, challenge, and proficiency examinations; contribute to curriculum development for the program; and take part in departmental service. Qualifications: Required qualifications: Master’s degree or higher (or foreign equivalent) in Chinese language pedagogy, teaching Chinese as a second/foreign language, Chinese linguistics, applied linguistics, or a closely related discipline, completed prior to the start date. At least two years of experience teaching Chinese at the college or university level, including documented teaching assistantships of record. Experience teaching Chinese at more than one level of the curriculum. Experience with technology-enhanced instruction and with a learning management system. Superior or near-native proficiency in Mandarin Chinese (ACTFL Superior or equivalent), including the ability to read and teach both simplified and traditional characters. Professional proficiency in English sufficient to teach, advise, and conduct departmental business. Evidence of teaching effectiveness. Preferred qualifications: Doctorate in Chinese language pedagogy, Chinese linguistics, applied linguistics, second language acquisition, Chinese literature, or a related field. Experience teaching heritage learners and using differentiated instruction in mixed-ability classrooms. Training or demonstrated practice in proficiency-oriented and performance-based instruction; ACTFL OPI/WPT familiarity or certification. Experience with curriculum design, materials development, and common assessment across sections. Experience coordinating multi-section courses or mentoring teaching assistants and part-time instructors, and contributing to co-curricular and program-building work. Special notes: This is an in-person appointment at the West Campus, governed by the Policies of the SUNY Board of Trustees and the State/UUP agreement. The successful candidate must be eligible to work in the United States and must complete a pre-employment background check. Application Instructions: All application materials must be submitted online. Please use the Apply Now button to begin your application. A complete application consists of a cover letter addressing the qualifications; a CV; a statement of teaching philosophy of two pages or less addressing learners at different levels, including heritage learners; evidence of teaching effectiveness; one sample syllabus and one sample lesson plan; and three professional references, from whom letters will be requested at the semi-finalist stage. Application Process: This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. https://apply.interfolio.com/193424 For further information about this position, please contact Professor Yi Wang at yiw.wang@stonybrook.edu . For questions regarding the application process, please contact Ms. Theresa Spadola at t heresa.spadola@stonybrook.edu or call 631-632-7690. Stony Brook University/SUNY is an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192098": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Modern U.S. Social and Cultural History",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: History Location: Stony Brook, New York The History Department at Stony Brook University (State University of New York) is delighted to invite applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of History, specializing in Modern U.S. Social and Cultural History. Preferred candidates will have expertise in the history of society and culture in the modern U.S. and/or the modern U.S. in the world. We particularly encourage applicants whose scholarship encompasses topics related to immigration and/or gender and sexuality, race, and ethnicity. We also prefer applicants with hands-on experience in public history, museum work, and/or the digital humanities. The successful candidate will be prepared to contribute to the History graduate program’s thematic clusters , teach a wide range of topics (including survey courses and specialized seminars for undergraduate and graduate students), and actively contribute to building community within the History Department and across the university. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to graduate student mentorship, recognizing positive student-faculty relations as essential towards creating an inclusive departmental culture, as well as advancing student achievement in career placement, publications, and research. The successful candidate will have opportunities to collaborate with other interdisciplinary entities at Stony Brook (such as the Humanities Institute of Stony Brook, the Center for Changing Systems of Power, and the Institute for Globalization Studies). Required qualifications: Ph.D. in History (or related field) in hand by July 1, 2027; relevant archival research experience. Preferred qualifications: 1.      Specialization in Modern U.S. Social and Cultural History; 2.      Expertise in topics related to immigration and/or gender, race, and ethnicity; 3.      Ability to teach undergraduate surveys and seminars as well as graduate courses. A completed initial application will include: Cover letter (please no longer than 4 pages), describing scholarly research agenda, teaching experience, and activities relevant to the preferred criteria specified above. CV Writing sample (chapter or journal article of 25-40 pages) List of three references with e-mail contact information Only references of shortlisted candidates will be contacted. Shortlisted candidates will be expected to provide a research statement and teaching statement with sample syllabus, additional chapters (if ABD) and possibly other materials. The Department expects to conduct remote interviews with shortlisted candidates and then host finalists on campus in early 2027. Review of applications will begin on November 15, but additional applications will be accepted until December 1, 2026. Campus Description: Stony Brook University is an R1 research institution located on the north shore of Long Island (60 miles east of New York City) and is a flagship campus of the State University of New York (SUNY) system. The vibrant student body is one of the most diverse found at any research institution throughout the United States. Stony Brook University is also one of the best schools in the nation for promoting economic and social mobility. The University embraces its mission to provide comprehensive undergraduate, graduate and professional education of the highest quality, and its rankings reflect that mission. Stony Brook University is the #1 public university in New York, #26 among public univeristys according to U.S. News and World Report 2024 Best Colleges. SBU is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities, composed of the top 62 research institutions in North America. As Long Island’s largest single site employer, Stony Brook has nearly 15,000 full- and part-time employees, including more than 2,800 faculty and nearly 26,000 students  — 17,600 undergraduate students and 8,300 graduate students — and offers more than 200 majors, minors and combined-degree programs. Located approximately 60 miles east of Manhattan on Long Island’s beautiful North Shore, Stony Brook is situated on 1,454 wooded acres, encompassing 13 schools and colleges; a Research and Development Park; world-class athletics facilities, including an 8,300-seat stadium and a 4,000-seat arena; and Stony Brook Medicine, Long Island’s premier academic medical center. Also part of the University is a teaching and research campus in Southampton, New York, which offers graduate arts programs and is the site of the Marine Sciences Center. In addition, Stony Brook has a role in running, and performs joint research with, Brookhaven National Laboratory, the only Department of Energy Laboratory in the Northeast. Home to the Emerson String Quartet, the Pollock-Krasner House in East Hampton, NY, and the Humanities Institute, with endeavors that extend to the Turkana Basin Institute in Kenya and the Ranomafana National Park in Madagascar, Stony Brook sustains an international reputation that includes the arts, humanities, social sciences and natural sciences. About the College: As part of a great research university, the College of Arts and Sciences at Stony Brook University incubates creative work and scholarship in fundamental disciplines, connecting with medicine, technology, public policy, culture, education, the arts, business and environment. As the liberal arts college for the campus, we help students and faculty explore diverse possibilities that exceed their initial expectations and prepare for a lifetime of learning and discovery.  http://www.stonybrook.edu/cas/ The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632- 6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193794": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor-Theoretical Phonology, Linguistics",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: Linguistics Location: Stony Brook, New York, United States The Department of Linguistics at Stony Brook University invites applications for an Assistant Professor position in phonology beginning Fall 2027. The successful candidate will have a PhD (or foreign equivalent) in Linguistics (or related field) in hand by the start of the appointment, an active research program in theoretical phonology, and prior teaching experience preferably in phonology. Applicants with additional expertise in areas such as field research, phonetics, specialized language areas, computational, experimental, and mathematical approaches to the study of language are especially encouraged to apply. The ideal candidate is expected to: Maintain an active research program in theoretical phonology which addresses core concerns of the field and complements and enhances the Department’s strengths, and apply for grant/fellowship funding. Teach graduate and undergraduate courses in phonology and contribute to the teaching of the Department’s core curriculum and the development of the Department’s graduate and undergraduate degree programs. (The usual teaching load is 2 courses per semester.) Advise and mentor a diverse student body of graduate and undergraduate students, and supervise student research. Required: Doctoral degree (or foreign equivalent) in Linguistics (or related field) in hand by the start of the appointment Specialization and active research program in theoretical phonology Prior teaching experience Preferred: Additional expertise in related areas, including, but not limited to, field research, phonetics, specialized language areas, computational, experimental, and mathematical approaches to the study of language. Prior teaching experience in phonology Documented teaching excellence Experience in advising and mentoring students Prior application for grant and/or fellowship funding To apply, submit a cover letter, CV, description of research program, representative research samples, teaching statement, and three letters of recommendation through Interfolio by November 21, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192636": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor - Department of Mathematics, College of Arts and Sciences",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: Mathematics Location: Stony Brook University The Mathematics Department at Stony Brook University expects to have one position at the rank of Associate or Full Professor available beginning in the fall semester of 2027 contingent on funding availability.  ,,oWe seek candidates with outstanding research achievements and potential, as well as a strong commitment to teaching. Candidates must have a PhD in mathematics and have held a tenured position or equivalent. Candidates must have an excellent publication record. A requirement of the position includes teaching of undergraduate and graduate mathematics classes, and a commitment to departmental and university service. Applicants in all fields are encouraged to apply. Along with mathematicians working in areas of traditional interest in the department – which include geometry and topology in many flavors, complex and real analysis, dynamical systems, partial differential equations, mathematical physics, number theory, probability and mathematics education – we are also interested in building in other areas, including algebra. Required Qualifications: Candidates must have a PhD (or foreign equivalent) in mathematics. Candidates must have an excellent publication record. Candidate must have held a tenured position or equivlent. Teaching of undergraduate and graduate mathematics classes. A commitment to departmental and university service. Applicants should complete the Interfolio Application Process online at: (Supply Interfolio Link Here). Application materials include: 1) AMS Standard Cover Sheet, 2) Cover Letter, 3) CV, 4) Publication List, 5) Research Statement, 6) Teaching Statement, and 7) At least three reference letters addressing Research and one addressing Teaching. Preference will be given to applications received by November 15, 2026 Stony Brook University is committed to creating and maintaining a workplace and educational environment that is safe, accessible, and free of all forms of discrimination, sexual misconduct or research misconduct, among other infractions. In support of this commitment, certain candidates for employment will be required to disclose such employment-related misconduct findings and pending investigations or proceedings, and final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions will authorize their current and previous employer(s) from the last seven (7) years to disclose such information to the University. Employment is contingent on your full and complete disclosure on these matters. In the event that you fail to disclose any such matter or in the event of an unsatisfactory outcome of the disclosure and review process, an offer of employment may be revoked at SBU's sole discretion. If SBU becomes aware of a failure to disclose or misrepresentation of any such matter after your employment commences, you may be subject to discipline, up to and including termination. The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632- 6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194108": {
      "title": "2027 Simons Lecturer, Department of Mathematics, College or Arts and Sciences",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: Mathematics Location: Stony Brook University The Mathematics Department at Stony Brook University expects to have two Simons Instructor (SUNY title of Lecturer) positions available beginning in the fall semester of 2027 contingent on funding availability. A Simons Instructor position is a three-year post-doctoral level appointment with a competitive salary of $75,000 and reduced teaching. Required Qualifications: Applicants are required to have earned a Ph.D (or foreign equivalent)degree in Mathematics before 8/31/27. Preferred Qualifications: It is preferred that applicants received their Ph.D. (or foreign equivalent) in 2020  or later, and that their research areas compliment those of current department members. Application materials include: 1) AMS Standard Cover Sheet, 2) Cover Letter, 3) CV, 4) Publication List, 5) Research Statement, 6) Teaching Statement, and 7) At least four Reference Letters. Please be sure to apply to the following positions in the Simons Center for Geometry and Physics and the Institute for Mathematical Sciences as well: Research Assistant Professor - The Simons Center for Geometry & Physics has two open positions for Mathematics Research Assistant Professors available with a starting date of September 1, 2027 These are non-tenure track appointments offering an annual salary of $85,000. A research fund of $5,000 will be offered for scientific travel, inviting visitors and other research related expenses on a yearly basis; in addition, $2,000 will be provided to defray moving expenses in the first year. The Simons Center for Geometry and Physics is an endowed research organization devoted to furthering fundamental knowledge in geometry and theoretical physics; especially knowledge at the interface of these two disciplines.  Please click here to apply. https://apply.interfolio.com/193704 Milnor Lecturers - The Institute for Mathematical Sciences (IMS) at Stony Brook University seeks applications for one postdoctoral position (Milnor Lecturer) starting in the Fall 2027 semester. The Milnor Lecturer is a three-year appointment with a competitive salary of $70,000 and reduced teaching. Please click here to apply. https://apply.interfolio.com/193727 IMPORTANT NOTICE: All applicants must complete their application in Interfolio. Required Qualifications: Applicants are required to have earned a Ph.D (or foreign equivalent) degree in Mathematics before 8/31/27. Preferred Qualifications: It is preferred that applicants received their Ph.D. (or foreign equivalent) in 2020 or later, and that their research areas compliment those of current department members. Complete applications should include the following: 1) AMS Standard Cover Sheet 2) Cover Letter 3) CV 4) Publication List 5) Research Statement 6) Teaching Statement 7) At least four Reference Letters. IMPORTANT NOTICE: All applicants must complete their application in Interfolio. The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632- 6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195002": {
      "title": "2027 Hefei/Stony Brook Lecturer - (Non Tenure Track Position)",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: Mathematics Location: Stony Brook University Stony Brook University has a unique three-year opportunity in mathematics available with a start date of September 1, 2027. This is a non-tenure track appointment offering a salary of $75,000. We are seeking candidates with outstanding research potential. The first year of this position, starting September 1, 2027, will be spent in Hefei, China employed with The Institute for Mathematics and Fundamental Physics . The following two years, starting September 1, 2028 will be spent employed with the department of Mathematics at Stony Brook University in New York. The teaching load for this position will be 1 course during your first year while you are in Hefei and 5 courses during your second two years while you are at Stony Brook University. Please be sure to apply to following positions within the mathematics community at Stony Brook. Mathematics Department:  Simons Lecturer - https://apply.interfolio.com/194108 Simons Center for Geometry and Physics - https://apply.interfolio.com/193704 The Institute for Mathematics Sciences - https://apply.interfolio.com/193727 Required Qualifications: Applicants are required to have earned a Ph.D (or foreign equivalent) degree in Mathematics before 8/31/27. Preferred Qualifications: It is preferred that applicants received their Ph.D. (or foreign equivalent) in 2020 or later, and that their research areas compliment those of current department members. Applicants should complete their application at https://www.stonybrook.edu/jobs/ through Interfolio untilizing this link Apply  Application materials include: 1) AMS Standard Cover Sheet 2) Cover Letter 3) CV 4) Publication List 5) Research Statement 6) Teaching Statement, and 7) At least four Reference Letters. Applications should be received by November 20, 2026. IMPORTANT NOTICE: All applicants must complete their application in Interfolio. The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632- 6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190819": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Department of Neurobiology and Behavior",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: Provost-Neurobiology and Behavior Location: Stony Brook, New York The Department of Neurobiology and Behavior is searching for neurobiologists with expertise and strong scientific interests in cellular neuroscience for a tenure-track position at the Assistant Professor level. Candidates whose research focuses on membrane physiology, synaptic signaling, cellular physiology and/or biophysics are especially encouraged to apply. We are also interested in researchers who use advanced imaging and/or other functional approaches to investigate cellular and synaptic function. A research program that connects fundamental mechanisms of cellular physiology to healthy brains and/or disease models will be considered a strength. Our Department is home to a diverse and collaborative group of basic and translational researchers. We seek exceptional and creative candidates with a PhD or MD/PhD, or foreign equivalent degree to establish and lead an innovative, laboratory-based research program. We expect candidates to have at least two years of postdoctoral research experience after their degree and demonstrate strong academic credentials in a specific research field. Teaching experience is also preferred for participation in the departmental educational mission and teaching assignments. Ideal candidates will supervise postdoctoral fellows, graduate students, and undergraduate students in research, drawing on the broad range of expertise within our faculty for support and collaboration. The successful candidates will join a large, diverse, inclusive and outstanding community of neuroscientists at Stony Brook University. New faculty will be actively mentored in our exceptionally collaborative and nurturing environment, which features highly productive research programs, state-of-the-art equipment, and outstanding core facilities. Stony Brook offers a highly competitive salary and benefits package. All departmental faculty are members of the Graduate Program in Neuroscience, and Medical Scientist Training Program and their laboratories will be housed in either the Life Sciences Building or the adjacent Centers for Molecular Medicine Building, commensurate with their interests and expertise. Required Qualifications: A PhD or MD (or foreign equivalent degree) with at least 2 years of postdoctoral experience after degree in neuroscience or related discipline. Preferred Qualifications: Expertise in cellular neuroscience including membrane physiology, synaptic signaling, cellular physiology and/or biophysics with an emphasis on disease states. All application materials must be submitted online. Please use the Apply Now button to begin your application. For technical support, please visit Interfolio's Support Site ( https://support.interfolio.com/ ) or reach out to their Scholar Service Team at help@interfollio.com or (877) 997-8807. Applicants should submit the following documents: a letter of application (‘cover letter’) a curriculum vitae with a list of publications a statement of research interests a description of teaching interests and mentoring philosophy a diversity statement at least three letters of reference to: Neurobiology Search, SUNY Stony Brook at Interfolio using the “Apply Now” button. (https://facultysearch.interfolio.com/15355/positions/188927) . The review of applications will begin November 30th, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. For questions regarding this position, please contact Grace Rossano at 631-632-4766. The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632- 6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police . Stony Brook University is committed to creating and maintaining a workplace and educational environment that is safe, accessible, and free of all forms of discrimination, sexual misconduct or research misconduct, among other infractions. In support of this commitment, certain candidates for employment will be required to disclose such employment-related misconduct findings and pending investigations or proceedings, and final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions will authorize their current and previous employer(s) from the last seven (7) years to disclose such information to the University. Employment is contingent on your full and complete disclosure on these matters. In the event that you fail to disclose any such matter or in the event of an unsatisfactory outcome of the disclosure and review process, an offer of employment may be revoked at SBU's sole discretion. If SBU becomes aware of a failure to disclose or misrepresentation of any such matter after your employment commences, you may be subject to discipline, up to and including termination."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194425": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Early Modern Philosophy",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: Philosophy Location: Stony Brook University We seek a candidate with a record of research and teaching that complements the Department of Philosophy’s strengths in contemporary Continental thought and the history of philosophy. The successful candidate will be expected to design and teach a range of undergraduate courses, in addition to, specialized graduate seminars. We are particularly interested in applicants committed to enriching Stony Brook’s lively philosophical community. Responsibilities include undergraduate and graduate teaching (2/2 teaching load), dissertation and thesis supervision, and standard non-teaching duties. Salary and benefits are competitive. Required Qualifications: ● Ph.D. at the time of appointment ● Demonstrated experience teaching philosophy courses at the undergraduate level ● Ability to design and teach graduate courses in Early Modern Philosophy ● Articulated research agenda Candidates should submit a complete dossier including a cover letter; CV; sample of academic writing; outline of current and future research interests; graduate transcripts; three letters of reference; and a teaching portfolio containing evidence of teaching effectiveness and sample graduate and undergraduate syllabi. Stony Brook University invites applications from individuals whose experiences, perspectives, and commitments will contribute to a vibrant academic community. We value a wide range of backgrounds and encourage candidates who bring unique insights that advance excellence, innovation, and access in higher education. Deadline for applications is November 9th, 2026. All application materials must be submitted online via Interfolio. For technical support, please visit Interfolio Support Site (https://support.interfolio.com/) or reach out to their Scholar Service Team at help@interfollio.com or (877) 997-8807. For questions regarding this position, please contact the Philosophy Department at alissa.betz@stonybrook.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193127": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Observational Astronomy (tenure-track)",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: Physics and Astronomy Location: Stony Brook University Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Observational Astronomy The Department of Physics & Astronomy at Stony Brook University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Observational Astronomy, broadly defined, with an anticipated start date of Fall 2027. We are seeking applicants specializing in areas of observational astronomy, particularly in the fields of exoplanets or transient phenomena. Candidates whose research directions complement and enhance existing strengths in the department are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will be expected to establish a vigorous, externally funded research program characterized by originality and long-term vision, while also contributing to excellence in teaching and mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. The new hire will join the Astronomy Group within the Department of Physics & Astronomy. The group has a strong tradition of research excellence, spanning the study of the early universe with CMB observations, constraining dark matter and dark energy, and studying galaxy properties and their evolution, exotic compact objects, and planetary systems outside of the solar system. Faculty of the Astronomy Group are actively involved with many facilities including the Rubin, Roman, JWST, LIGO, and Simons Observatories. They also have close connections to the C.N. Yang Institute for Theoretical Physics (YITP), the Institute for Advanced Computational Science (IACS), Brookhaven National Laboratory, and the Center for Computational Astrophysics (CCA) at the Flatiron Institute. These connections offer rich opportunities for collaboration and joint initiatives. The person holding this position will report to the Chair of the Physics and Astronomy Department. During the academic year the faculty member may be asked to give seminars on their work and is expected to present their research at national and/or international conferences. The faculty member will teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses in the Department of Physics and Astronomy and will be expected to encourage, and as appropriate participate in, the research and mentorship of graduate students and postdoctoral associates. The faculty member will participate in departmental, university and professional service. Position Requirements Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: ● PhD in astronomy or a related field. ● Demonstrated ability to perform independent research. ● Demonstrated track record of publication of research in high-quality peer-reviewed journals and delivery of presentations at major conferences. ● Clear and concise oral and written communication, interpersonal, and analytical skills. Preferred Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: ● Expert knowledge of techniques of observational astronomy, broadly defined . ●        Post-PhD work experience in observational astronomy. ● Research expertise in a field that complements and enhances existing strengths in the department. ● Previous mentoring and teaching experience. ● Experience working with a broad student population and the ability to foster academic excellence, collaboration, and a respectful learning environment within the Department and University Applications should be submitted through the Interfolio web service. Required materials include: ● Cover letter ● Curriculum vitae ● Research statement ● Teaching philosophy statement In addition, applicants should arrange for at least three letters of reference to be submitted through Interfolio. For full consideration, applications should be received by 11/30/26 ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193405": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Biological Physics (tenure-track)",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: Physics and Astronomy Location: Stony Brook University Faculty Job Description The position is a joint appointment between the Department and the Laufer Center for Physical and Quantitative Biology. The tenure home of the person holding this position is in the Department of Physics and Astronomy and the faculty member will report to the Chair of the Physics and Astronomy Department. During the academic year the faculty member may be asked to give seminars on their work and is expected to present their research at national and/or international conferences. The faculty member will teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses in the Department of Physics and Astronomy and will be expected to encourage, and as appropriate participate in, the research and mentorship of graduate students and postdoctoral associates. The faculty member will participate in departmental, university and professional service. The successful candidate will be expected to establish a vigorous, externally funded research program characterized by originality and long-term vision, while also contributing to excellence in teaching and mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. The new hire is expected to actively participate in the Department’s expanding Biological Physics program, including being a mentor in the Department’s Biological Physics PhD concentration, and also collaborate closely with members of the Laufer Center. The Laufer Center has strong research programs in molecular biophysics, cell biology, network biology, synthetic biology, drug discovery and the application of AI in biophysics. In addition, there are opportunities to collaborate with the departments of Biochemistry & Cell Biology, Applied Math & Statistics, Neurobiology & Behavior and Chemistry, as well as the C.N. Yang Institute for Theoretical Physics (YITP), the Simons Center for Geometry and Physics (SCGP), and major facilities at Brookhaven National Laboratory. These connections offer rich opportunities for joint initiatives. Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: ●        PhD in Physics, Biophysics, Chemical Physics or a related field. ●        Demonstrated ability to perform independent research. ●        Demonstrated track record of publication of research in high quality peer-reviewed journals and delivery of presentations at major conferences. ●        Clear and concise oral and written communication and interpersonal and analytical skills. Preferred Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: ●        Expert knowledge in relevant theoretical or computational physics, biophysics or mathematical modeling of biological systems on the cell or molecular level. ●        Research expertise in a field that complements and enhances existing strengths in the department. ●        Previous mentoring and teaching experience. · Experience working with a broad student population and the ability to foster academic excellence, collaboration, and a respectful learning environment within the Department and University. ·         Experience supporting students from varied backgrounds and a demonstrated commitment to advancing teaching, research, and service in ways that strengthen the academic community. Applications should be submitted through the Interfolio web service. Required materials include: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research statement Teaching philosophy statement Diversity statement In addition, applicants should arrange for at least three letters of reference to be submitted through Interfolio. For full consideration, applications should be received by 11/16/26 ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194923": {
      "title": "Experimental Quantum Information Systems (QIST) Tenure Track Associate/Full Professor, Empire Innovation",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: Physics and Astronomy Location: Stony Brook University The Department of Physics & Astronomy ( http://www.physics.sunysb.edu/Physics/ ) at Stony Brook University invites applications for a tenured faculty position at the associate/full professor level in experimental physics within the area of Quantum Information Science and Technology (QIST). The successful candidate will hold a PhD in Physics or a closely related field, demonstrated record of research leadership in a QIST-relevant experimental physics research area with a preference for candidates having carried out research using ion trapping methods. Faculty are expected to engage in independently funded research characterized by creativity and vision, with commitment to the teaching missions of the Department of Physics and Astronomy, including our Master of Science program in QIST. Existing relevant research efforts in the Department include ultracold atoms and quantum gases, ultrafast dynamics of molecules and materials, photonic quantum devices, quantum optics, quantum communication and entanglement distribution, and quantum materials. The successful applicant will complement and expand on our efforts in these areas. We are particularly interested in applicants with expertise in using ion trapping systems for quantum information processing to complement our work in the development of large-scale sensing systems that are enabled by long-distance quantum networks. T his position is part of a larger university plan for a major investment in the QIST area, which would include considerable resources to support QIST-relevant research across the University and specifically within the Department of Physics and Astronomy. Applications will be made through the Interfolio web service (Job ID: 193441) They should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, copies of up to three recent papers, a research statement, a statement of teaching philosophy, and names and affiliations of at least three references. The candidate should use the options provided at the Interfolio website to request the letters of reference. The review of applications will begin on October 30, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. The programs in physics at Stony Brook are characterized by highly ranked faculty, excellent students, and close affiliation with experimental and theoretical research centers of international reputation, including the Simons Center for Geometry and Physics, the C.N. Yang Institute for Theoretical Physics, the Institute for Advanced Computational Science, the Flatiron Institute and Brookhaven National Laboratory. We welcome applications from highly qualified candidates with a broad range of backgrounds, experiences, perspectives,  and scholarly interests. We seek colleagues who will contribute to the academic excellence of our department and to Stony Brook University’s mission of advancing research, education, discovery, and public service. Stony Brook University is New York State’s flagship public research university and one of the nation’s leading research institutions. A member of the State University of New York (SUNY) system and the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU), Stony Brook is classified as an R1 institution, reflecting the highest level of research activity. With nationally recognized strengths in STEM, health sciences, medicine, and interdisciplinary research, Stony Brook offers faculty a dynamic and highly collaborative environment for discovery, teaching, and innovation. Our location on Long Island also provides access to the extraordinary academic, scientific, cultural, and professional opportunities of the greater New York metropolitan region. Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: ● Ph.D. in physics or a related subject. ● Research in experimental QIST ● At least 5 years of faculty-level research or equivalent experience ● Clear and concise oral and written communication and interpersonal and analytical skills. Preferred Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: ● Research using ion trapping ● Active research funding ● Established outreach program ● Demonstrated Ph.D. student supervision ● Experience specifying and commissioning their laboratory ● Experience working with a broad student population and the ability to foster academic excellence, collaboration, and a respectful learning environment within the Department and University ·         Experience supporting students from varied backgrounds and commitment to advancing teaching, research, and service in ways that strengthen the academic community. Applications will be made through the Interfolio web service (Job ID: 193441) They should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, copies of up to three recent papers, a research statement, a statement of teaching philosophy, and names and affiliations of at least three references. The candidate should use the options provided at the Interfolio website to request the letters of reference. For full consideration the application materials must be received by 11/30/26."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190638": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - SUNY PRODiG Plus - Department of Psychology, College of Arts & Sciences",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: Psychology Location: Stony Brook, NY The Department of Psychology in the College of Arts and Sciences at Stony Brook University seeks to hire and mentor a new faculty member through the Promoting Recruitment, Opportunity, Diversity, Inclusion and Growth Plus ( PRODiG+ ) Postdoctoral Fellowship Program. PRODiG Plus is designed with two primary goals: increasing the number and share of excellent diverse faculty committed to advancing the ideals of diversity, equity, and inclusion; strengthening the pipeline for retention and support of those faculty. Additional information on the PRODiG Plus Fellowship can be found at https://www.suny.edu/prodig-plus/fellowship/ . The position is a full-time, 12-month, non-tenure-track faculty position at the lecturer level to commence in Fall 2027. The successful candidate will be appointed for 2 years at an annual salary of $80,000 with comprehensive state benefits. The PRODiG Plus fellow will be part of a mentored research environment, with funds to support research and a modest teaching load (3 courses total over two years). The position is intended to support a productive post-doctoral research period. At the completion of this two-year fellowship position, fellows with an upward trajectory of scholarly achievement and a demonstrated equitable and inclusive teaching strategies focused on student success will be invited to join the tenure-track faculty at Stony Brook University through an internal promotion process. The PRODiG Plus Fellow will join the Department of Psychology in one of our four program areas: clinical psychological science, cognitive science, integrative neuroscience, or social and health. We are seeking a Fellow whose academic scholarship, preferably, complements the department’s existing strengths in health disparities, lifespan development, and/or affective/cognitive science. We are particularly interested in candidates who demonstrate a commitment to inclusive excellence and facilitating an equitable, supportive, and welcoming academic environment for all students, including those who have experienced barriers to higher education; excellence in innovative research (with the potential for funding), and potential to participate in the undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring mission of the psychology department. Required Qualifications: Be eligible to work in the United States for the full duration of the position without a visa sponsorship ( Note: if you currently need sponsorship or will need it in the future to maintain employment authorization, you do not meet the eligibility requirements for this position ); Have a PhD or relevant terminal degree from an accredited institution completed prior to start date; Demonstrate personal, academic, and/or work experience engaging with diversity, equity, and inclusion and/or a commitment to facilitating and enhancing diversity, equity and inclusion efforts in the campus community.  Such experiences may include, but is not limited to, an academic/scholarly track record focused on diversity, equity, and inclusion; work, volunteer/unpaid/community service, or related experience/expertise in serving underserved or vulnerable areas and/or populations. Preferred Qualifications: A record of publication(s), preferably relevant to health disparities, lifespan development, and/or affective/cognitive science. Evidence of, or potential for funding. Ability/potential to teach and mentor students at both the graduate and undergraduate levels. Preference will be given to candidates who are: • Current SUNY students, alumni, and/or Fellows. • From a low-income background (e.g., Pell-eligible as undergraduates), were first-generation college students (students whose parents have not earned a bachelor’s degree), have overcome adversity, are AmeriCorps alumni, or are veterans. The PRODiG Plus Fellowship is open to any and all prospective candidates regardless of race, color, national origin, or sex, and is consistent with all current governing federal and state nondiscrimination law. All application submissions will be assessed and evaluated in accordance with the required eligibility criteria. Please use the Apply Now button to begin your application. A complete application will consist of the following items, including the required application form: Cover letter CV Statements of research (past, current, and future directions, max 1,000 words) and teaching interests (your experience, future teaching interests, teaching and mentorship style, max 1,000 words); 3 representative publications Three letters of reference An essay in response to the following in 750 words or less: “Please describe how you’ve engaged, facilitated, and/or enhanced diversity, equity and inclusion efforts in the campus community. Examples could include personal, academic, and/or work experience and may include, but is not limited to, an academic/scholarly track record focused on diversity, equity and inclusion; work, volunteer/unpaid/community service; or related experience/expertise in serving underserved or vulnerable areas and/or populations.” For technical support, please visit Interfolio's Support Site (https://support.interfolio.com/) or reach out to their Scholar Service Team at help@interfollio.com or (877) 997-8807. For questions regarding this position, please contact Cynthia Forman at psychology_search@stonybrook.edu Special Notes: The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632- 6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police. About Stony Brook University Stony Brook is New York State’s flagship public research university and has been ranked the #1 public university in New York by US News & World Report in recent years. It is part of the State University of New York (SUNY) system, a center of academic excellence and an internationally recognized research institution that offers all students a world-class education. Stony Brook University is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU) and is classified as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Foundation, reflecting the highest level of research activity. As a leading institution in STEM, health sciences, and interdisciplinary research, Stony Brook offers faculty a rich, collaborative environment."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195171": {
      "title": "Lecturer",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Arts & Sciences: Writing and Rhetoric Location: Stony Brook University S tony Brook University’s Department of Writing and Rhetoric (DWR) is hiring a full-time lecturer specializing in English Language Learning (ELL, ESL, ESOL, ENL) to start in August 2027. Possession of an MA in TESOL or Applied Linguistics at the time of application; minimum three years’ experience teaching English language learners (ELLs) in a postsecondary setting, including but not limited to grammar, phonetics, and/or phonology; proficiency in prescriptive English grammar and phonetics; and native or native-like fluency in English are required. Preferred credentials include experience with group-based rubric assessment of student learning outcomes; evidence of academic service; and demonstrated commitment to advancing educational access, opportunity, and inclusive excellence. The position offers a two-year, renewable appointment for 3 in-person courses per fall/spring semester, teaching undergraduate ELLs in the Program in Academic English, graduate-level international TA training, and first-year writing in the DWR. Other duties include performing departmental service and mentoring students. Required Qualifications Possession of an MA in TESOL or Applied Linguistics at the time of application Minimum three years' experience teaching English language learners (ELLs) in a postsecondary setting, including but not limited to grammar, phonetics, and/or phonology Proficiency in prescriptive English grammer and phonetics Native or native-like fluency in English Preferred Qualifications Experience with group-based rubric assessment of student learning outcomes Evidence of academic service Demonstrated commitment to advancing educational access, opportunity, and inclusive excellence All application materials must be submitted online through Interfolio. Please use the Apply Now button to submit the following documents: CV Application letter explaining your qualifications, teaching philosophy, and teaching effectiveness 3 confidential letters of recommendation Sample syllabus and materials from your course(s), at least focusing on written language and optionally focusing on oral/aural communication (or another distinct content/skill area) For this position, we are unable to sponsor candidates for work visas. Inquires should be directed to: writingdepartment@stonybrook.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187888": {
      "title": "SUNY PRODiG Plus Fellow, Mechanical Engineering- College of Engineering and Applied Sciences",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Provost Office: College of Engineering and Applied Sciences: Mechanical Engineering Location: Stony Brook University Stony Brook University invites applications for a SUNY Promoting Recruitment, Opportunity, Diversity, Inclusion, and Growth plus ( PRODiG Plus ) Postdoctoral Fellowship Program in the Department of Mechanical Engineering. This fellowship is a full-time, 12-month, non-tenure-track faculty position at the lecturer level and will commence in Fall 2026. The PRODiG Plus program is designed with two goals: (1) increasing the number and share of excellent diverse faculty committed to advancing the ideals of diversity, equity, and inclusion; and (2) strengthening the pipeline for retention and support of these candidates. Additional information on the PRODiG Plus Fellowship can be found at https://www.suny.edu/prodig-plus/fellowship/ . The fellow will be a part of a mentored research environment throughout the two-year long fellowship. With successful performance and evaluation, candidates who demonstrate excellence in innovative research (with the potential for funding), equitable and inclusive teaching strategies focused on student success, and potential to participate in the undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring mission of the mechanical engineering department, will be invited to join the tenure-track faculty at Stony Brook University at the end of the fellowship. Required Qualifications: Eligible to work in the United States without a visa sponsorship PhD (or foreign equivalent) in Mechanical Engineering or related field from an accredited institution prior to the start of employment D emonstrate personal, academic, and/or work experience engaging with diversity, equity, and inclusion and/or a commitment to facilitating and enhancing diversity, equity, and inclusion efforts in the campus community. Such experience may include, but is not limited to, an academic/scholarly track record focused on diversity, equity, and inclusion; work, volunteer, unpaid, community service; or related experience or expertise in serving underserved or vulnerable areas and/or populations. Preferred Qualifications: Evidence of, or potential for, excellence in research and service. Undergraduate and graduate teaching experience. Demonstrated proficiency in fostering interdisciplinary collaboration across diverse fields. Preference will be given to candidates who are: Current SUNY students, alumni, and/or Fellows. From a low-income background (e.g., Pell-eligible as undergraduates), were first-generation college students (students whose parents have not earned a bachelor’s degree), have overcome adversity, are AmeriCorps alumni, or are veterans. The PRODiG Plus Fellowship is open to any and all prospective candidates regardless of race, color, national origin, or sex, and is consistent with all current governing federal and state nondiscrimination laws. All application submissions will be assessed and evaluated in accordance with the required eligibility criteria. All application materials must be submitted online. Applications received by July 10 , 2026 will receive full consideration. Candidates who apply on or after July 10, 2026 will be considered on a rolling basis until the position is filled. The below documents are required and must be submitted through Interfolio. Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Research Statement Teaching Statement An essay in response to the following in 750 words or less: “Please describe how you've engaged, facilitated, and/or enhanced diversity, equity, and inclusion efforts in the campus community. Examples could include personal, academic, and/or work experience and may include, but is not limited to, an academic/scholarly track record focused on diversity, equity, and inclusion; work, volunteer/unpaid/community service; or related experience/expertise in serving underserved or vulnerable areas and/or populations.” Names and contact information for four references All application materials must be submitted online. Please use the Apply Now button to begin your application. For technical support, please visit Interfolio's Support Site (https://support.interfolio.com/) or reach out to their Scholar Service Team at help@interfollio.com or (877) 997-8807. The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632- 6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190414": {
      "title": "Anesthesiologist, Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Anesthesiology, Regional Anesthesia",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Health Sciences: Renaissance School of Medicine: Anesthesiology Location: Stony Brook, New York The Renaissance School of Medicine, Stony Brook University, Department of Anesthesiology is recruiting for an Anesthesiologist with a Fellowship in Regional Anesthesia. Stony Brook University is located 60 miles from New York City on Long Island’s scenic North Shore. Our 1,100-acre campus is home to a diverse community comprised of 25,710 undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students and more than 13,500 faculty and staff, including those employed at Stony Brook Medicine, Suffolk County's only academic medical center and tertiary care provider. Long Island Lifestyle: Excellent schools Beautiful Beaches and waterfront communities Short train access to Manhattan Reasonable cost of living compared to New York City We are committed to recruiting and retaining exceptional anesthesiologists by providing: Competitive compensation package (Base Salary and Calls): Competitive Base Salary. Additional compensation for Regional Anesthesia Fellowship Certification. Overtime Pay. Potential for Extra Shift Compensation. Performance-based quality incentives. Additional compensation for academic productivity. Practice allowance to cover conferences, licenses, and CME expenses. ASA membership covered by Department. Non-clinical time: Dedicated academic time. Day off post-call followed by late coverage. Vacation Time. Sick days, Conference Meetings PTO. Comprehensive benefits package. Robust retirement programs including New York State pension, 401, 403. Excellent Work life balance. Department strongly supports Academic Promotions. Academic and Clinical Excellence The successful candidate will join a collaborative team dedicated to innovation in regional anesthesia, perioperative medicine, and acute pain management. Responsibilities include: Providing anesthesia care for a diverse and complex surgical population. Main focus will be performing regional anesthesia procedures, Acute Pain Service, and Orthopedic Anesthesia. Participating in the acute pain calls. Teaching and mentoring medical students, anesthesiology residents. Opportunities to develop programmatic, educational, and research initiatives within regional anesthesia and acute pain medicine. Stony Brook Medicine is Suffolk County's largest academic medical center and a major tertiary referral center serving Long Island and the surrounding region. Stony Brook Medicine includes multiple hospitals, ambulatory surgery centers, and outpatient facilities, providing comprehensive care across a broad spectrum of surgical and medical specialties. The Department of Anesthesiology provides care across operating rooms, numerous non-operating room anesthesia locations, a high-volume obstetric anesthesia service, and several ambulatory surgery centers. Faculty enjoy exposure to a diverse and complex case mix, including trauma, cardiac, neurosurgical, transplant, orthopedic, vascular, pediatric, and obstetric patients. Stony Brook University Hospital is a Level I Trauma Center and has been nationally recognized among America's 100 Best Hospitals for Joint Replacement™, Stroke Care™, and Cardiac Care™. General Responsibilities: The candidate will provide anesthetic care to patients undergoing surgery or other procedures and will provide consultation before and after the anesthetic procedure. Perform regional anesthesia and be part of the acute pain services team. The candidate will be actively engaged in the instruction and/or supervision of medical students, resident physicians and certified registered nurse anesthetists. The Regional Anesthesia and Acute Pain Service is a high-volume, academically focused program that provides 24/7 consultative and clinical coverage. Faculty practice includes advanced ultrasound-guided regional anesthesia across a broad range of surgical specialties and plays a key role in perioperative pain management and enhanced recovery pathways.  Faculty are supported by a robust infrastructure that includes dedicated nursing support, advanced practice providers, trainees, administrative resources, and research personnel, allowing them to focus on clinical excellence, teaching, innovation, and professional growth. Join a growing and collegial team of regional anesthesiologists committed to advancing regional anesthesia, acute pain medicine, education, and research. Opportunities exist for program development, future fellowship, quality improvement initiatives, and clinical research. Required Qualifications MD or DO (or foreign equivalent) degree Licensed, or, eligible for licensure in New York State Completed an ACGME accredited residency program in Anesthesiology Board Certified in Anesthesiology Completed/soon to complete a Fellowship in Regional Anesthesia All application materials must be submitted online. Please use the Apply Now button to begin your application. For technical support, please visit Interfolio's Support Site ( https://support.interfolio.com/ ) or reach out to their Scholar Service Team at help@interfolio.com or (877) 997-8807. For questions regarding this position, please contact Mr. Armando Santos at Armando.Santos@stonybrookmedicine.edu Special Notes: Tenure/Non-Tenure Track position. FLSA Exempt position, not eligible for the overtime provisions of the FLSA. To qualify for tenure and/or a senior faculty appointment, the candidate must meet the criteria established by the School of Medicine ( School of Medicine's Criteria for Appointment, Promotion and Tenure ). Anticipated Start Date: As soon as possible. Campus Description: Long Island's premier academic medical center, Stony Brook Medicine, represents Stony Brook University's entire medical enterprise and integrates all of Stony Brook's health-related initiatives: education, research and patient care. It encompasses Stony Brook University Hospital, Stony Brook Children's Hospital, the five Health Sciences schools -- Dental Medicine, Health Professions, Medicine, Nursing and Social Welfare -- as well as the major centers and institutes, programs and more than 50 community-based healthcare settings throughout Suffolk County. With 624 beds, Stony Brook University Hospital serves as Suffolk County's only tertiary care center and Regional Trauma Center. Stony Brook Children's, with more than 180 pediatric specialists in 30 specialties, offers the most advanced pediatric specialty care in the region. In the Medical and Research Translation (MART) building, two floors are occupied by Stony Brook University Cancer Center’s outpatient services, and four floors are devoted to cancer research. Diversity, equity and inclusion are essential core values at Stony Brook Medicine. We believe we do our best and most impactful work when we leverage our diverse, equitable and inclusive perspectives. We are proud to recruit and hire talented people from a wide variety of backgrounds and experiences. The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632- 6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193717": {
      "title": "Anesthesiologist, Clinical Associate/Full Professor, Anesthesiology, Vice Chair for Clinical Research",
      "description": "Department: Stony Brook University: Health Sciences: Renaissance School of Medicine: Anesthesiology Location: Stony Brook, NY, United States Stony Brook Medicine, Department of Anesthesiology has a Vice Chair for Clinical Research faculty position available at the Associate Professor/Professor Level. Tenure vs. non-tenured depending upon experience and qualifications. Stony Brook University is located 60 miles from New York City on Long Island’s scenic North Shore. Our 1,100-acre campus is home to a diverse community comprised of 25,710 undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students and more than 13,500 faculty and staff, including those employed at Stony Brook Medicine, Suffolk County's only academic medical center and tertiary care provider. General Responsibilities : The candidate will provide anesthetic care to patients undergoing surgery or other procedures and will provide consultation before and after the anesthetic procedure. The candidate will be actively engaged in the instruction and/or supervision of medical students, resident physicians and certified registered nurse anesthetists. Vice Chair for Clinical Research: Candidate must have a strong track record of peer-reviewed funding in Clinical or translational research, a national/international reputation for research leadership, mentorship, and the ability and desire to develop a strong funded clinical research program. The successful candidate will also have a track record of working with and receiving industry support (pharmaceutical and/or device trials). Duties will include participation and mentorship in departmental activities that pertain to undergraduate, graduate, post-graduate, and faculty research and education. As a member of the executive team, the candidate will be expected to assist in strategic planning and direction, play an important role in administrative aspects pertaining to the initiation of grant support, design of research projects, and organizational aspects related to the research goals of the entire department. The successful candidate will also have the ability and desire to mentor junior clinical faculty to participate and develop their own research projects leading to potential funding, presentations, abstracts and published manuscripts. We are searching for outstanding candidates with an innovative research program and a history of extramural (Government i.e., NIH, private foundations and industry) pharmaceutical/device manufacturer funding. Candidates must have history of prior or current research and extramural funding. History of publications in high impact journals.  Successful candidates are expected to bring their own vigorous dynamic program that would synergize with the ongoing studies at the Department. The Department and the School of Medicine have excellent research infrastructure, which includes MRI, PET, CT, microscopy, behavioral, genomics and proteomics equipment and facilities. Existing Research Resources and Support: The basic and clinical science divisions of the Department have recently undergone major growth and restructuring, with an expanded focus on basic and clinical neuroscience. There are 7 federally funded basic science investigators and some clinical faculty with research and clinical trials experience. Clinical Research Support includes 4 clinical research coordinators and a full-time statistician. Research interests include, but are not limited to, molecular mechanisms of neurodegenerative disorders and chronic pain, adult neurogenesis, learning and memory, brain imaging, mitochondrial dysfunction, ciliopathies, and genomics, investigated on models ranging from Drosophila and frogs to mice and humans. Required Qualifications: MD or DO (or foreign equivalent) degree Completed an ACGME accredited residency program in Anesthesiology Board Certified in Anesthesiology Licensed, or, eligible for licensure in New York State Proven documented scholarly activities, publications, and research Minimum of 10 years of postgraduate experience with active research funding Preferred Qualifications: MS or PhD (or foreign equivalent) in a basic science or related area Proven Leadership training and experience, successful track record of industry funding A three-page research plan addressed to: Dr. Syed Shah, MD Professor and Chairman Department of Anesthesiology All application materials must be submitted online. Please use the Apply Now button to begin your application. For technical support, please visit Interfolio's Support Site ( https://support.interfolio.com/ ) or reach out to their Scholar Service Team at help@interfolio.com or (877) 997-8807. For questions regarding this position, please contact Mr. Armando Santos at Armando.Santos@stonybrookmedicine.edu Special Notes: Tenure/Non-Tenure Track position. FLSA Exempt position, not eligible for the overtime provisions of the FLSA. To qualify for tenure and/or a senior faculty appointment, the candidate must meet the criteria established by the School of Medicine ( School of Medicine's Criteria for Appointment, Promotion and Tenure ). Anticipated Start Date: As soon as possible. Campus Description: Long Island's premier academic medical center, Stony Brook Medicine, represents Stony Brook University's entire medical enterprise and integrates all of Stony Brook's health-related initiatives: education, research and patient care. It encompasses Stony Brook University Hospital, Stony Brook Children's Hospital, the five Health Sciences schools -- Dental Medicine, Health Professions, Medicine, Nursing and Social Welfare -- as well as the major centers and institutes, programs and more than 50 community-based healthcare settings throughout Suffolk County. With 624 beds, Stony Brook University Hospital serves as Suffolk County's only tertiary care center and Regional Trauma Center. Stony Brook Children's, with more than 180 pediatric specialists in 30 specialties, offers the most advanced pediatric specialty care in the region. In the Medical and Research Translation (MART) building, two floors are occupied by Stony Brook University Cancer Center’s outpatient services, and four floors are devoted to cancer research. Diversity, equity and inclusion are essential core values at Stony Brook Medicine. We believe we do our best and most impactful work when we leverage our diverse, equitable and inclusive perspectives. We are proud to recruit and hire talented people from a wide variety of backgrounds and experiences. The selected candidate must successfully clear a background investigation. In accordance with the Title II Crime Awareness and Security Act, a copy of our crime statistics is available upon request by calling (631) 632- 6350. It can also be viewed online at the University Police website at http://www.stonybrook.edu/police ."
    },
